# RME route keys and failure-log redaction (rule 222)

**Status:** GO scope of `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-1` (R-01, R-02) and `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-2` (R-03..R-08),
both 2026-11-05. Sprint records: `docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-1.md`,
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-2.md`. Owner approval: each revision below, recorded in its sprint doc
(CG-22). Policy: rule 221 §7 (RME revision programme). Category under the Foundation-First Sprint Lock: hotfix /
security / monitoring, all allowed before Foundation GO; the lock itself is unchanged.

## 1. R-01, R-03..R-08 — a route key that is not a database key never reaches the database

1. These parameters are constrained globally by `App\Support\Routing\DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN`. Each is
   domain-specific and every route carrying it binds a bigint `id()` primary key implicitly (no model overrides the
   route key; there is no explicit `Route::bind`):

   | Parameter | Routes | Key | Revision |
   |---|---|---|---|
   | `clinicVisit` | 44 | `trx_clinic_visits.id` | R-01 (Program 1) |
   | `rmeInvoice` | 15 | `trx_rme_invoices.id` | R-03 |
   | `medicalRecord` | 7 | `trx_medical_records.id` | R-04 |
   | `rmePrescription` | 4 | `trx_rme_prescriptions.id` | R-05 |
   | `consent` | 3 | `trx_rme_visit_consents.id` | R-06 |
   | `odontogram` | 3 | `trx_odontograms.id` | R-07 |

   The pattern is `[1-9][0-9]{0,17}`: 1–18 digits, no sign, no leading zero. Every key an identity column issues is in that
   range, and 18 nines is below the bigint ceiling, so PostgreSQL can always cast what the router lets through.
2. Why: implicit model binding runs `where "id" = '<segment>'`. On PostgreSQL a non-integer segment raises
   SQLSTATE 22P02 and a number beyond bigint raises 22003, so the request answered HTTP 500 and wrote an ERROR line
   instead of a 404. SQLite compares loosely and finds no row, so the defect is invisible on SQLite. It was live in
   production, where every such line holds the monitoring log signal at WATCH.
3. `DatabaseRouteKey::register()` runs first in `AppServiceProvider::boot()`. A global pattern applies only to
   routes defined after it is registered; the compiled collection that `route:cache` writes carries it.
4. **Never add a per-route constraint for a parameter `DatabaseRouteKey` governs.** A route-level `where` /
   `whereNumber` OVERRIDES the global pattern. Four visit-bound legacy-archive routes carried
   `->whereNumber('clinicVisit')` (`[0-9]+`), which still admits a number beyond bigint; they were removed. One
   authority per parameter.
5. Extending the global constraint to another DOMAIN-SPECIFIC parameter means adding its name to
   `DatabaseRouteKey::CONSTRAINED_PARAMETERS`; nothing else. Before adding one, prove from the code that every route
   carrying the name binds a bigint key, and that no route overrides it.
6. **A GENERIC parameter name is never constrained globally** (`visit`, `patient`, `record`, `page`, `branch`, …): it
   may later name another identifier type, and a global numeric pattern would silently 404 that route. The oversight
   lookup already resolves a visit by its string visit number. Constrain a generic name per route with
   `->where('<name>', DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN)`, reusing the constant (never a copied regex), and never with
   `whereNumber`, whose `[0-9]+` admits a number beyond bigint or PHP's integer range plus a leading-zero alias.
7. R-08: the five `oversight/rme/visits/{visit}` routes now carry `->where('visit', DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN)`.
   They are not model-bound: the controller takes `int $visit`. Under `whereNumber` a number beyond PHP's integer
   range failed the `int` conversion (TypeError, HTTP 500 plus ERROR), and `0<id>` cast to the real id, so a
   non-canonical URL opened — and audited — a real clinical record. 18 digits always fits a PHP integer.
8. **Implicit binding runs BEFORE the route's permission middleware.** So the unconstrained defect was reachable by
   any authenticated account, including one holding no permission, and a well-formed key that names no record is a
   404 from binding for everyone (the permission gate only ever sees a real record). The constraint keeps both
   orders intact: real record without permission → 403, no such record → 404, non-key segment → 404. Do not
   "fix" the binding order to make a permission 403 appear first.
9. A tightened constraint also tightens `PostAuthenticationRedirectService::matchGetRoute()`: an intended URL that
   no longer matches a route falls back to the default landing. That is the intended direction.
10. Still open (measured 2026-11-05, NOT approved, NOT done): RME-area parameters that remain unconstrained or use
    `whereNumber` — among them `{diagnosis}` (7 routes, none) inside the medical-record routes,
    `{receiptSnapshot}` (`whereNumber` + `int` argument) on the receipt reprint route, and the other oversight
    parameters (`{patient}`, `{handwriting}`, `{handwritingPage}`, `{record}`, `{page}`, all `whereNumber`). Each
    needs its identifier type verified and its own owner approval. Inventory: `docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-2.md`.

## 2. R-02 — a caught exception is logged through the canonical redactor

1. Log a caught exception's text only through
   `App\Modules\Observability\Support\TelemetryRedactor::exceptionMessage()`, plus the exception class. Never
   `$e->getMessage()` raw into a log context.
2. Why: a `QueryException` message ends with ` (Connection: …, SQL: …)` with every binding interpolated, and
   PostgreSQL adds `DETAIL: Key (col)=(value)` / `Failing row contains (…)` lines carrying row values. A raw message
   can therefore write clinical values into the application log (CG-11). The redactor keeps the driver's error text
   (SQLSTATE included) and strips the rest.
3. Applied to the post-payment lab-candidate failure warning in `RmePaymentService`. That failure is still logged
   and still never rolls the payment back (Sprint 21 Phase 21.2, unchanged).
4. Reuse the redactor; never write another message sanitiser. A second copy drifts: the older
   `Str::before(…, ' (Connection:')` pattern strips the SQL tail but not the DETAIL row.
5. Limit, stated not hidden: the redactor removes quoted literals, NIK/phone digit runs, Nomor RM and dates; it
   cannot recognise an UNQUOTED free-text name in a non-database message. No current throw site under lab-candidate
   generation produces one. A new throw site that interpolates patient text must quote it or not include it.

## 3. Regression protection

- `tests/Feature/RME/ClinicVisitRouteKeyConstraintTest.php`: every `{clinicVisit}` route and the compiled
  (cacheable) collection carry the pattern; guests and signed-in users get 404 with no ERROR for a non-key segment;
  the legacy-archive routes keep no looser override; real keys still work.
- `tests/Feature/RME/RmeRecordRouteKeyConstraintTest.php` (R-03..R-07): the global list is exactly the six names and
  excludes `visit`; every route carrying the five parameters, the compiled collection and Symfony's cached matcher
  carry the pattern; 13 non-key segment classes answer 404 with no ERROR for a guest and (one segment per test — a
  failed statement aborts the PostgreSQL transaction) for a signed-in user; the permission order of §1.8.
- `tests/Feature/ClinicOversight/OversightVisitRouteKeyConstraintTest.php` (R-08): the five routes carry the
  canonical pattern (live and compiled); overflow and the leading-zero alias are 404 with a positive control; the
  in-branch visit still opens, an out-of-branch one is 404, the reader permission gate is unchanged.
- `tests/Feature/RME/RmePaymentLabCandidateFailureLogPrivacyTest.php`: no binding, DETAIL row, SQL tail or synthetic
  clinical value in the warning; SQLSTATE and exception class kept; payment not rolled back.
- All four are declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites` and selected in BOTH critical
  variants (`ClinicVisitRouteKey`, `RmeRecordRouteKey`, `OversightVisitRouteKey`, `RmePayment`). The behavioural 404/500 cases are load-bearing on PostgreSQL 16,
  which the critical gate uses.
